[agent:result] name: hops-fg
[agent:result] description: Use when writing Python code that creates, inserts into, or manages tables or feature groups. Auto-invoke when user writes feature pipelines, feature engineering code, or asks about feature group best practices (online vs offline, batching, OOM, materialization, embeddings, statistics).
[agent:result] ---
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] When a user refers to tables, clarify that you interpret them as feature groups in Hopsworks.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] # Hopsworks Feature Groups — Python SDK Best Practices
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Writes computed features into a Hopsworks feature group — the storage backing the F (feature) stage of the FTI pattern.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] A feature pipeline applies **model-independent transformations (MITs)** and writes the resulting **untransformed, reusable** feature data to feature groups. Do NOT store model-dependent transformations (MDTs — e.g. scaling, one-hot encoding) in a feature group: those are applied later, in the feature view, when reading for training/inference. Storing MDT output makes the data non-reusable across models, can cause write amplification (a parameterized MDT like standardization rewrites every existing row), and breaks EDA on raw values. Reuse is the payoff: the lowest-cost feature pipeline is the one you don't have to create, so write features other models can also select.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Contract
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] - **Input:** a DataFrame (Pandas/Polars/PySpark) of computed features, plus the target name/version and key columns.
[agent:result] - **Output:** a feature group registered server-side (on first insert) and populated with rows; optionally online-enabled and materialized to the offline store.
[agent:result] - **Pre-condition:** a Hopsworks project login and a feature store handle (`fs = project.get_feature_store()`); any parent FGs used for provenance already exist.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Smoke-test (cheap pre/post-flight)
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Before writing Python, and to confirm results after, use the CLI. No Spark session needed:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```bash
[agent:result] hops fg list                              # is the name/version free? did it register? (note the STORE column)
[agent:result] hops fg info <name> --version 1           # metadata: id, online flag, primary key, event_time
[agent:result] hops fg features <name> --version 1       # schema with primary-key / partition flags
[agent:result] hops fg preview <name> --version 1 --n 10 # first rows (flag is --n, not -n)
[agent:result] hops fg preview <name> --columns a,b,c    # project away wide embedding/array columns
[agent:result] hops fg stats <name> --version 1          # null counts / ranges — spot bad data early
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] To preview an FG in a shared store from the CLI, pass `--featurestore <store>`
[agent:result] (the STORE value from `hops fg list`).
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] `hops fg list` shows a **STORE** column. Imported / public feature groups live in a
[agent:result] **shared** store, not this project's own — that distinction matters when you read
[agent:result] them (see "Reading from a shared store" below).
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] A feature group is registered server-side on its **first insert**, not at `get_or_create_feature_group(...)`. Until the first insert `fg.id` is `None` and `hops fg list` will not show it.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Ask the user (before writing a feature pipeline)
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Before creating a feature group, clarify these decisions with the user:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] 1. **Online or offline?**
[agent:result]    - **Offline only** (`online_enabled=False`, `stream=False`): writes directly to Delta Lake. Best for batch training data, historical analytics, large-volume cold storage.
[agent:result]    - **Online + offline** (`online_enabled=True`, `stream=True`): writes go to Kafka → RonDB (online), then a Spark materialization job copies to Delta (offline). Required for low-latency serving, real-time feature lookups, and feature views with `get_feature_vector()`.
[agent:result]    - Default to offline unless the user needs online serving.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] 2. **Does this FG derive from other FGs?** If so, pass `parents=[fg1, fg2, ...]` at creation time. This sets up explicit provenance/lineage tracking in the Hopsworks UI. Always pass the actual FeatureGroup objects, not names.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] 3. **Data volume** — estimate row count × column count × avg bytes per value. This drives decisions on batching, statistics, and materialization (see below).
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] 4. **Time-series or not?** If features change over time, set `event_time` to the timestamp when the feature value was *valid* (not when the row was ingested). This is what lets the feature store build point-in-time correct training data (no future leakage, no stale values) via the feature view. Omit `event_time` only for immutable feature data.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ---
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Feature data types & online-store constraints
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Pick a supported type up front: a write with an unsupported type fails, and retrying the same type just loops.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Supported feature types:**
[agent:result] - Scalars: `int`, `bigint`, `float`, `double`, `boolean`, `string`, `date`, `timestamp`, `binary`.
[agent:result] - Composite: `array<type>` and `struct<field:type,...>` — e.g. `array<float>`, `struct<lat:double,lon:double>`.
[agent:result] - `decimal` is **not** supported. Use `double`, or `string` when you need exact precision.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Online store (`online_enabled=True`):**
[agent:result] - Scalars map straight to RonDB. Strings become `varchar(n)`, auto-sized to the longest value seen (rounded up to 100) and widened on later inserts; very long text falls back to `text`.
[agent:result] - Composite types (`array`, `struct`) **do** write online — they are stored Avro-encoded and decoded by the SDK on read. An online FG with an `array<float>` column is fine; you do not need to drop or flatten it.
[agent:result] - For **similarity search**, declare the vector as `array<float>` **and** attach an `EmbeddingIndex` (see Vector Embeddings): the FG is then backed by the vector DB (OpenSearch) instead of RonDB. Without an embedding index an `array<float>` is stored data, not a searchable index.
[agent:result] - Online is an upsert: one row per primary key, a new write for an existing key overwrites it.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Let the schema be inferred from the DataFrame when you can; pass an explicit `features=[Feature(name, type, ...)]` list only to pin a type (e.g. `bigint` over an inferred `int`, or an `array<float>` embedding column).
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ---
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Creating a Feature Group
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```python
[agent:result] import hopsworks
[agent:result] from hsfs.feature import Feature
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] project = hopsworks.login()
[agent:result] fs = project.get_feature_store()
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] # Get parent FGs for provenance (if this is a derived FG)
[agent:result] parent_fg = fs.get_feature_group("source_table", version=1)
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] fg = fs.get_or_create_feature_group(
[agent:result]     name="my_feature_group",
[agent:result]     version=1,
[agent:result]     description="Clear description of what this FG contains",
[agent:result]     primary_key=["id_col"],
[agent:result]     event_time="event_ts",             # enables time-travel queries
[agent:result]     features=[                         # explicit schema (recommended)
[agent:result]         Feature("id_col", "bigint", description="..."),
[agent:result]         Feature("amount", "double", description="..."),
[agent:result]     ],
[agent:result]     online_enabled=True,               # True for online serving
[agent:result]     stream=True,                       # True when online_enabled=True
[agent:result]     parents=[parent_fg],               # provenance lineage
[agent:result]     statistics_config=False,           # see "Statistics" section below
[agent:result]     # offline_backfill_every_hr=4,     # see "Materialization" section below
[agent:result] )
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] **Always describe what you create.** Pass `description=` on the feature group and a `description=` on every `Feature(...)`. A feature group or column with no description shows as an empty envelope in the UI and is not discoverable in search. If the user gave none, write concise ones from what each feature means; never leave them blank.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ### Key Parameters
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] | Parameter | When to use |
[agent:result] |---|---|
[agent:result] | `online_enabled=True` | Need online feature serving or real-time lookups |
[agent:result] | `stream=True` | Always set when `online_enabled=True`; enables unified write API |
[agent:result] | `parents=[...]` | FG is derived from other FGs — pass list of parent FG objects |
[agent:result] | `statistics_config=False` | Large data volumes (see Statistics section) |
[agent:result] | `event_time="col"` | Need time-travel queries or incremental reads |
[agent:result] | `embedding_index=EmbeddingIndex(...)` | FG contains vector embeddings (see Embeddings section) |
[agent:result] | `offline_backfill_every_hr=N` | Schedule automatic materialization every N hours |
[agent:result] | `ttl=timedelta(days=30)` | Auto-expire old rows from online store |
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ---
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ## Inserting Data
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ### Simple Insert (default for most cases)
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```python
[agent:result] fg.insert(df, wait=False)  # async — returns immediately
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] - Accepts: Pandas DataFrame, Polars DataFrame, PySpark DataFrame, NumPy array, or Python list
[agent:result] - `wait=False` (default): returns immediately; ingestion runs in background
[agent:result] - `wait=True`: blocks until online ingestion AND offline materialization complete
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ### When to Use `wait=True`
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Use `insert(df, wait=True)` when:
[agent:result] - **Low on CPU/memory**: `wait=True` for online FGs ensures only one Spark materialization job runs at a time. Multiple concurrent async inserts can each launch a Spark job, exhausting cluster resources.
[agent:result] - **Pipeline ordering matters**: downstream steps depend on data being fully materialized.
[agent:result] - **Debugging insert failures**: async mode may silently swallow errors.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ```python
[agent:result] # Safe pattern for resource-constrained environments
[agent:result] fg.insert(df, wait=True)
[agent:result] ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] ### Batch / Multi-Part Insert (for large datasets)
